[Music]  elon musk may be the superstar ceo of  spacex  but a lot of the credit goes to the guy  who was instrumental in making the  rockets  fly i'm tom mueller i'm actual founder  of the company tom mueller may not be  famous like musk but he's a big part of  the reason the company is as successful  as it is today  as vice president of propulsion and  later the propulsion chief technology  officer  he led the design testing and  construction of spacex's engines  before joining spacex mueller developed  engines at trw  a large aerospace company in los angeles  later acquired by northrup grumman  as a hobby he spent weekends testing and  flying his own machines in the mojave  desert  with an amateur rocket club called  reaction research society  that's how he met musk almost 20 years  ago through the club  the future spacex ceo had been looking  to recruit a bright team of engineers to  build rockets  the year before musk had gone to moscow  to try to buy refurbished icbms  but when the russians wanted too much  money he set out to build his own rocket  for less so when it came time to  founding spacex  musk saw mueller someone who knew  everything there was to know about  rocket engines  when i met elon it's like wow i'd really  like to go do this thing it's  high risk it's where i want to be going  to a startup was risky but also gave him  free reign to do more  mueller is known for developing rocket  engines from scratch  including the merlin engine this is a 95  000 pound thrust engine basically this  engine will burn a hole right through  concrete  these engines power the falcon line of  rockets the 27 in the falcon heavy  generate over 5 million pounds of thrust  at liftoff and the falcon 9 gets its  name from the nine merlin engines in the  first stage  they're fueled by rocket-grade kerosene  and liquid oxygen  it's not easy developing an engine that  can withstand a 170 second burn  long enough to send a payload into orbit  and then returned safely  to earth merlin revolutionized space  flight as it's designed to be reused  building reusable rockets put spacex on  the map  it's now a common sight to see the  falcon 9 first stage perform one of its  signature landings last year  that rocket launched astronauts aboard  the crew dragon to the international  space station  mueller also developed the engines that  helped the dragon spacecraft maneuver  while in orbit  well before these achievements mueller  was right beside musk  during the company's most difficult days  when attempts to launch its first rocket  the falcon 1 kept failing  the third time it tried to fly into  orbit the first stage rear-ended the  second  caused by a small amount of residual  thrust from the first stage after the  engine was cut off  spacex had enough money to scrounge up a  fourth in what would have been  final flight if it failed this time the  team told the rocket to wait a few more  seconds after the engine was cut off  before stage separation  fourth time's a charm  the falcon 1 became the first privately  developed liquid fuel rocket to orbit  the planet  which led to a billion dollar contract  with nasa that saved the company  mueller has been an integral part of the  story of spacex from the start  but he almost never became an engineer  if it were not for some sage  advice as the son of a logging truck  driver raised in a small town in idaho  being an engineer wasn't something he  considered he wanted to be an aircraft  mechanic  then i took a math class when i was a  freshman in high school  and my math teacher said to me do you  want to be the guy  that fixes a plane or the guy that  designs the plane  that was a pivotal point of my life of  course i wanted to be the guy  that designs the plane no one had told  me that i had the aptitude to be an  engineer  i didn't even know what an engineer did  that teacher changed my life he spent  summers logging to pay his way through  college  studying mechanical engineering at the  university of idaho  after graduation he set out for  california in its thriving aerospace  industry  but instead of finding a job designing  planes he wanted to work on his  childhood passion  rockets i realized that was a key  decision  a fork in the road where i made the  right choice to do what i love  don't settle put yourself on the right  path and end up where you want to be  after a storied career at spacex he  stepped back into a part-time advisory  role in 2016  as musk in the propulsion department led  the development of new engines  for a new rocket  this could be the first engine to take  people to mars raptor engines power  spacex's fully reusable rocket  starship they're fueled by liquid oxygen  and liquid methane  methane could possibly be made on the  red planet for the journey home  as spacex soars to new heights much of  its achievements can be traced back to  the early work of tom mueller  in fact musk acknowledged his  contributions were critical to spacex's  success  the man who helped make the rockets fly  officially retired in late 2020  capping off a nearly two decade career  at spacex which has been in his words  quite a ride ability plus luck equals  success if you have the ability then  luck  is your opportunity mueller may have met  the right visionary at the right  time but it took more than luck to win  musk over  a great engineer requires a solid  background in math  science and computer science whether  you'd like to brush up on the math you  learned as an undergrad  want to take a programming or simply  wish to expand your critical thinking  through logic courses  you can sign up for free with brilliant  brilliant offers over 60  interactive courses there are no tests  and if you happen to answer incorrectly  my sponsor brilliant explains where you  went wrong you can register with the  link in my description  and the first 200 people to use my link  will get 20  off the premium subscription which gives  you unlimited access to all the courses  thanks so much for watching for news  think i'm cindy palm

